WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.380
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.284
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.284 --> 00:00:05.710
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.210
PROFESSOR: In this
goodie bag, we'll

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.250
be exploring atomic
packing and solids

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.197
through the cubic
Bravais lattices.

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:13.780
We'll make three ball-and-stick
models of different cubic unit

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.500
cells.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.860
For this, you will need
31 balls and 44 sticks--

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.970
24 for FCC, 12 for
SC, and 8 for BCC.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.610
If you don't have a fancy
crystal modeling kit like this,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.837
any ball-and-stick
equivalents will work.

00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:28.420
A delicious alternative
would be using

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.550
toothpicks and marshmallows.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.049
The objective of
the goodie bag is

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:33.550
to explore the
different structures

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.290
of crystalline materials.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.210
As you build the models,
consider how many

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.140
nearest neighbors an atom has
in an SC, a BCC, and an FCC

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:41.770
crystal.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.470
So now we're going to
construct our Bravais lattices.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.750
We'll start with simple cubing.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.870
Begin by taking 8 balls
and connecting them with

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.820
sticks such that each ball
sits at the corner of a cube.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.560
Then for body-centered
cubic, we're

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.800
going to start with a single
ball in the center of a cube

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.850
and connect to 8 other balls
at the corners with sticks.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.700
For face-centered
cubic, it's going

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.560
to be a little more involved.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.300
We'll start with the corner
of a cube and form tetrahedra

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.790
to other balls, which
themselves will form tetrahedra

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.100
to another corner of a cube.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.780
If we iterate
through this process,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.230
we eventually end up with a full
face-centered cubic lattice.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.630
We've now constructed
three ball-and-stick models

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.610
of cubic Bravais lattices.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.320
The simple cubic
lattice has atoms

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.290
at each of the 8 corners
of the unit cell.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.750
The body-centered cubic
lattice has an additional atom

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.320
in the body center
of the unit cell.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.190
And the face-centered
cubic lattice

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.710
has an additional atom
in each of the 6 face

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.260
centers of the unit cell.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.080
Each of these lattices has a
different coordination number.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.870
That is, an atom in each
lattice has a different number

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:55.910
of nearest neighbors.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.600
If we imagine these
atoms as hard spheres

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.737
rather than this
ball-and-stick model,

00:02:01.737 --> 00:02:03.320
we can see that each
lattice will also

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.840
have a different
atomic packing factor.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.430
That is, each lattice will have
a different volume of space

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.470
occupied in the unit cell.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.010
In general, the coordination
number of a lattice

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.500
correlates with atomic
packing factor, which itself

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.150
correlates with stability.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.390
That's why we don't often find
simple cubic metallic solids

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.060
in nature.

